468 1138 false 13194139533722 true] [17592186248468 1145 "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13940224/assets/a8e7894ea970c539ca59a73713fa15450f4361b3fc8bc22183e3fe84946411bd.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x)=-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 3) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{csc}⁡(-x+6\\pi )+1 & =4(-\\frac{7}{6})+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{14}{3}+1 \\\\  & =-\\frac{11}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533722 true] [17592186248469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248470 13194139533722 true] [17592186248469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248471 13194139533722 true] [17592186248469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248472 13194139533722 true] [17592186248469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248473 13194139533722 true] [17592186248469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186248474 13194139533722 true] [17592186248469 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533722 true] [17592186248469 1041 "selection" 13194139533722 true] [17592186248469 1104 #edb/ref 17592186248473 13194139533722 true] [17592186248469 1110 #uuid "42304c1a-a61e-5e12-afaf-ad02255c8a13" 13194139533722 true] [17592186248470 1034 "-\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139533722 true] [17592186248470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533722 true] [17592186248470 1111 #uuid "a8f8f874-f2fc-57c8-b581-d